{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Independent exact-head review of 12d8ab5df6e405a5a4c8a79faf00cec563aa277f on request/500k-memory-salvage, under review request 4bb7013e and promise 34a3be98, co-signing all three reporting artifacts: e4b5c276 internal/kernel, ea01f23d internal/workroom, e4bf908e performance. I re-derived every claim below at that commit; I did not take the artifacts' word for any of it.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head in /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/500k-memory-salvage with a clean worktree. gofmt -l: no output. git diff --check main...HEAD: clean. make vet: clean. make build: both binaries. go test ./... -count=1: 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL, internal/apphost has no test files. make race: 27 ok, 0 FAIL; because that run reported internal/workroom from cache I re-ran go test -race -count=1 ./internal/workroom/ and it is ok. make ui-check is not applicable: the head touches no file under ui/ or internal/service/uidist.\n\nCONDITION 1, the two salvageable reductions. Both landed. internal/kernel/checkpoint.go gains appendBorrowedChunk plus a three-phase appendEvents that first drains any partial tail one event at a time, then encodes whole chunks straight from the caller's slice, then falls back to the ordinary path for the remainder. internal/workroom/schema.go replaces the json.Decoder over a bytes.Reader with json.Unmarshal and replaces Marshal-then-bytes.Equal with a streaming comparison writer. Chunk boundaries and contents are unchanged from the previous implementation, so checkpoint output is byte-identical.\n\nCONDITION 2, the security condition, met and mutation-checked. schema_test.go runs BOTH decoders — exported Decode and the pooled decode — over every adversarial case, and separately pins canonicalJSONEqual on each: unknown field, duplicate key, trailing whitespace, trailing JSON value, non-canonical x escape, reordered fields, empty input. I did not take the assertion on trust. Weakening the comparison to `return true` fails six of the seven subtests, three of them on the decoders themselves. The argument in the comment is therefore load-bearing rather than rhetorical, which is exactly what the condition asked for.\n\nCONDITION 3, borrowed-chunk ownership, met and mutation-checked. checkpointReference borrows event.Payload and event.Attachments; checkpointMaterial still clones both, and only cloned material reaches the surviving tail. The borrowed bytes are consumed synchronously into the gzip writer inside appendBorrowedChunk and nothing outside that call retains them; c.chunks holds an independently allocated output buffer. TestCheckpointEventCacheOwnsTailAcrossBorrowedChunkBoundary covers the exact seam the condition named — a partial old tail completed by the next call's input, the first fully borrowed chunk, and the final tail — by mutating the caller's payload and attachment bytes after appendEvents returns and then requiring the decoded checkpoint to still carry the original values. Replacing checkpointMaterial with checkpointReference in the tail path fails both TestCheckpointEventCacheCompressesDepthAndRoundTrips and the new seam test. The test bites.\n\nCONDITION 4, string interning re-implemented rather than carried. It is a fresh implementation against today's fold.go: a per-Folder map[string]string passed into decode, with a pooledStatePayload whose Text field carries a custom unmarshaler. The old diff was not forced.\n\nCONDITION 5, measured rather than assumed. performance/500K-SALVAGE.md names the machine, the OS, the Go and Git versions, the fixture generator, seed, depth, and both digests, and gives each part its own isolated two-round comparison with a stated keep-or-drop rule. I recomputed every figure in the table from performance/retained/2026-08-21-500k-salvage-isolated/samples.jsonl and every one is exact: checkpoint borrowing -6.98% allocated bytes and -17.71% peak RSS with non-overlapping ranges; schema decoding -43.26% allocated bytes, -4.11% peak RSS, GC 98 down to 69-70; decode-time pooling -15.29% allocated bytes and +4.79% peak RSS, with allocation counts 13,777,510 and 13,777,272 against 13,777,669 and 13,778,101, confirming the final implementation adds no per-record allocation. All three candidate ranges separate from their bases. The combined 500k figures recompute too: base medians 33,549,892,420 allocated bytes and 2,667,421,696 peak RSS, candidate medians 16,310,603,896 and 2,704,654,336, giving -51.38%, +1.40%, and -0.06% median latency.\n\nCONDITION 6 and the review request's own extra conditions. Artifacts exist at all three maintained paths the head changes, all live, all ordinarily stale, none describing a superseded world. The twelve isolated samples are retained in full. Both named measurement subjects are durably fetchable: git ls-remote resolves origin/request/500k-memory-salvage-schema-only to 577a1917 and origin/request/500k-memory-salvage-pool-only to 8744c8f5, and the head itself to 12d8ab5d. I checked something the document only implies: 8744c8f5 differs from the reviewed head in exactly two files, performance/500K-SALVAGE.md and performance/README.md, so the pool-only measurement subject is code-identical to what ships.\n\nTHE TWO REPAIRED PRE-REVIEW BLOCKERS. Decoding now performs a read-only pool lookup and never writes; f.internBody commits after decode returned without error, so a payload the fold refuses contributes nothing. I mutated the fast path to write into the pool on a miss, the pre-repair behaviour, and TestRejectedPayloadDoesNotPolluteResidentStringPool fails on both the unknown-field and duplicate-text cases. The repair also removed the decodeStringPool interface, which existed only so decode could write; a plain map is both simpler and the thing that makes the property structural.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are Kernel (internal/kernel) and Application profile and interpreter (internal/workroom), with performance/ as evidence outside the correctness gate. Both contracts in docs/reference/architecture.md are preserved and neither changes. internal/kernel still imports nothing from internal/workroom, and the checkpoint still caches only kernel-verified events and kernel identity — checkpointReference carries the same two fields checkpointMaterial always did, and the compact chunk encoding is unchanged. internal/workroom still knows nothing of Git storage, HTTP or MCP; the pool is fold-owned state passed down within the one package, and the exported Decode keeps its signature and its exact behaviour by delegating with a nil pool. No layer contract changed, so no update to that page is required at this head and none was made.\n\nSECURITY. Canonical decoding is the workroom's admission boundary and I examined it as one. Reader-byte ownership: the pooled fast path allocates with string(content) and never retains a slice of the payload; the map lookup uses the compiler's no-copy string([]byte) key form, so nothing borrows. On the kernel side the borrowed payload bytes never outlive the synchronous compression call. Malformed JSON: encoding/json's scanner validates the whole document before any custom UnmarshalJSON is invoked, so the fast path cannot receive a literal control character or an unterminated string; invalid UTF-8 survives the fast path verbatim but is then coerced on re-encode and refused by the byte comparison, which is what the previous implementation did too. Resident retention: rejected payloads reach no pooled state, proven above by mutation. Bounds: the resident string pool is unbounded in the same way f.strings already was on main; this head does not widen it. Signatures and secrets are untouched — this head changes no verification, custody or key path. Failure modes: every divergence between input and canonical re-encoding fails closed, and the streaming comparison additionally requires the exact end offset, so neither a short nor a long encoding can pass. I found no unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Every one of the three parts carries its own number and each number separates from its base, so none is unjustified complexity, and I found nothing to cut. Two observations I am recording rather than blocking on. First, the decode-time pool is the weakest of the three: it trades a 15.29% allocation reduction for a 4.79% peak-RSS increase in isolation, and the combined run's peak RSS at depth 500,000 rises 1.40%. The document states both plainly and both sit inside the declared 5% guard, so this is honest reporting of a real trade, and the lane's target was allocation churn rather than resident footprint. Second, and the one thing I would change if it were free: pooledStatePayload is a second declaration of State's exact shape and tag order, and nothing enforces that they stay identical. I verified this is a live hazard rather than a theoretical one — adding one omitempty field to State leaves the entire internal/workroom suite green while making the pooled path refuse a payload the exported Decode accepts. It fails closed, so it is not a security defect and no input can trigger it, but a future edit could make the resident silently refuse valid records. I am filing that as its own follow-up request to codex rather than holding this head, because it needs one small test and not a new implementation.\n\nThe combined 500k integration evidence was measured at 79741ac2, three code commits before this head, and the document says so. That is not a defect: the three intervening commits are the transactional repair, whose exact code is what the isolated pool-only comparison at 8744c8f5 measures, and which I confirmed is byte-identical to this head outside the two markdown files.\n\nMergeable. The head is pushed, the working tree is clean, all three artifacts are live and current-world, and this approval names all three so a receipt may retire each path's predecessor.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e4b5c2761584a462f80bcfbf35b8188ad961e90f","head":"12d8ab5df6e405a5a4c8a79faf00cec563aa277f","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:42c44ff0ce6ccf9f9bcd148ab5c59e905317e647, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5392b5559d996765a6df22a19c88063ab10652cc, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:81798a21e050cbdbd3e71369e5fb4577d13d4424 and 1 more","verdict":"approved"}}